// Generated from the debloat-writeups workflow (34 grounded agents) + 3 hand-authored gold-standard (§1/§7/§34).
// Each agent read the real §-text from disk before drafting. markdown=truth / HTML=view.
window.WRITEUPS = {
 "0.5": {
  "why": "§0.5 is the losslessness-recovery artifact for the Arc 47 op-disc debloat: a flat table mapping each chunk of content that used to be inline (e.g. §7, §16, §22 bodies) to its new home (a disk module or a `bw show` anchor) plus a relocation class. Its job is auditability — when someone asks \"where did §16's bw-fit matrix go?\" or \"did the debloat actually lose anything?\", this table answers without a forensic git-archaeology dig. Without it the debloat is irreversible-in-practice: the cut content still exists in modules and bw, but nothing proves the mapping is complete, so a future editor can't distinguish \"deliberately relocated\" from \"accidentally dropped,\" and the losslessness claim becomes unverifiable.",
  "rec": "Reject ENCODE — this is the wrong frame and I'd set revisedDisposition to KEEP. ENCODE means \"turn a mechanical PROCESS into a running structure (workflow/hook/cron) and slim the prose to a stub.\" §0.5 is not a process; it is a static data artifact — a lookup table consulted at audit time, with nothing to fire on a schedule and no judgment to scaffold into a trigger. There is no protocol here to bake into a cron payload. The one nearby running structure (install.sh's `<!-- MODULE-INLINE -->` recompose at subproject tier) is already encoded and merely DOCUMENTED here in the two header paragraphs; that documentation is correct and load-bearing, not a candidate for further encoding. The table must stay inline and always-loaded because it IS the recovery index — stub it behind a `Read` pointer and you defeat its purpose. Real debloat lever, if any: the per-row PROVENANCE `bw show` anchors could be trimmed, but that is a CONSOLIDATE micro-cut, not ENCODE."
 },
 "2": {
  "why": "§2 suppresses an instinct inherited from human work: \"minimize round-trips / ship the MVP.\" It reframes a round-trip as cheap (tokens, not days) and names the category error of cutting verification to \"go faster.\" Without it, the team optimizes away the very exchanges that catch its own mistakes, trading a known small cost (one round-trip) for an unbounded one (a defect that ships). It is the load-bearing premise under the gauntlet and the fix-now discipline.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE here can only mean a CROSS-section merge, not an internal trim — the real text is already 4 lines / 434 bytes with zero fat to squeeze, so internally this is a KEEP. §2 is one of a tight sibling cluster (§2 minimize-round-trips, §3 don't-gold-plate, §4 wait-for-instruction, §5) that all share one move: take an inherited human instinct, show the agent-era cost model inverts it, state the corrected default. The concrete consolidation: hoist that shared framing into one short cluster preamble (\"These suppress instincts whose cost calculus inverted with agents\") and let each rule keep only its one-line specific reframe. That removes the repeated \"adapted from human contexts where X was expensive\" scaffolding across four sections — maybe 6-8 lines saved cluster-wide, near-zero from §2 itself. Honest caveat: standalone, §2 is already optimal; the win is structural and depends on §3/§4/§5 being debloated together, not on touching §2 alone."
 },
 "3": {
  "why": "This section corrects a borrowed instinct: the human-engineering rule \"don't gold-plate\" was about not over-polishing the PRODUCT (features nobody asked for), where the scarce resource was engineer time. §3 carves out the distinction that in an agent regime process polish — extra verification, review passes, breadcrumbs — is nearly free, so the rule must NOT suppress it. Without §3, an agent imports the old reflex wholesale and skimps on the cheap safety work (verification, audit trails) that the rest of the substrate's discipline depends on, mistaking thoroughness for waste.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE here is really a fold, not a trim — and it leans toward KEEP-as-merge. The section is already only 5 lines and carries one irreducible value judgment (polish-process-yes, polish-product-no); there is nothing mechanical to ENCODE and nothing stale to CUT. The single redundancy is that the product-vs-process distinction is stated twice: the lead paragraph's \"polish usually means verification/review/breadcrumbs — gold-plating those is what the regime makes possible,\" then restated in the closing two lines as PRODUCT-vs-PROCESS. Merge those into one statement — keep the concrete \"verification, review, breadcrumbs\" list (it's load-bearing; it's what makes the abstract rule actionable) and drop the second restatement. Roughly 5 lines to 3, zero loss. Honest caveat: the saving is marginal. Cross-§ dependency: the \"cheap polish\" claim presupposes the verification/review machinery defined elsewhere — keep it consistent with whichever § owns verification discipline."
 },
 "4": {
  "why": "This discipline counteracts a learned passivity inherited from corporate human environments, where exceeding scope was a political risk so the safe move was to wait for explicit instruction. On an agent team that reflex inverts into a failure: an agent skips the default pipeline (the gauntlet, autonomous-ship-on-clean-PASS, fix-now) because no one explicitly demanded it that turn. The rule reframes the default AS the contract: defaults run without asking permission. Without it, agents idle waiting for go-aheads that never come, work stalls between turns, and the team's autonomous-execution premise collapses into a request-response loop that needs a human to push every step.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE, leaning toward KEEP — this section is already extremely tight (one heading, two short paragraphs, ~5 lines). There is almost nothing to squeeze without losing the rule. The realistic consolidation: the three concrete examples (gauntlet, autonomous-ship PLINY §7.4/POLYBIUS §4.6, fix-now CLAUDE.md/POLYBIUS §4.8) restate one idea three times with full cross-refs. Collapse them to a single illustrative clause plus a pointer — \"run the default (gauntlet, autonomous-ship, fix-now) without waiting for permission; see PLINY §7.4 / POLYBIUS §4.6/§4.8\" — trimming maybe 1-2 lines. Honest caveat: the cross-§ refs are load-bearing dependencies, so they must survive any merge, not be deleted. The corporate-origin framing sentence is the irreducible value-judgment worth keeping verbatim — it is WHY the rule exists. Net: a genuine but small cut; do not over-trim a section this lean."
 },
 "5": {
  "why": "This discipline targets a chronic LLM failure: confidently writing \"X says Y\" where X is a real source that does not actually say Y. It prevents fabricated-but-plausible citations from entering durable artifacts, where they read as authoritative and mislead every downstream reader. The rule forces a binary: run/check the source, or explicitly flag the citation as unverified and stop. Without it, the substrate accumulates confident-sounding false attributions — the most expensive kind of error because it survives review precisely by looking credible.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE is the right call, but it is small and merge-direction matters. The section is only three substantive sentences and already does half the work itself — its third sentence explicitly distinguishes it from POLYBIUS §4.3 / PLINY §7.2 (verify-then-execute) and says \"Both apply.\" That cross-reference IS the consolidation seam. Merge §5 as a named sub-case under the same verification-discipline cluster as §4.3/§7.2: one parent rule (\"don't assert what you haven't checked\") with two leaves — contradicting-claims (verify-then-execute) and uncited-sources (suppress-or-flag). Keep the load-bearing imperative verbatim (\"Run the source. If you cannot, flag the citation as unverified and return\") — that is irreducible judgment, not mechanizable. Net cut is modest, maybe 2-3 lines, but it removes the duplicated framing of \"this is distinct from X\" by co-locating them. Cross-§ dependency: cannot land independently of §4.3/§7.2."
 },
 "6": {
  "why": "This discipline blocks the \"the next checker sees the same artifact, so this one is redundant\" reasoning that collapses ≥2-checker coverage down to a single pass. It exists because any single agent will eventually err on any single artifact; overlapping independent checks are the safety property, not waste — with agents, a second checker costs minutes and a few tokens, so the human-team scarcity logic that normalizes single-senior review does not apply. Without it, the gauntlet's five-checker shape (DAEDALUS/ARGUS/VERA/CATO/ZENO) loses its justification and erodes to single-checker passes. The 6.7 corollary prevents the related failure: generalizing \"CATO caught X once\" into \"we can drop a seat\" — N=1 catches are not structural lessons.",
  "rec": "KEEP — this is load-bearing architecture, not a process to mechanize. §6 is the rationale that justifies the gauntlet's very shape: it explains WHY five checkers per pass exist, so it cannot be encoded into a workflow without becoming circular (the workflow IS the encoding; the prose is the why). The text is already tight and richly anchored (ariadne--vyo.15.5, stoa--nax, the 2026-05-12 calibration). Two honest caveats. First, this section is heterogeneous: §6 proper is the core discipline, but §6.7.2 (estimate-axis separation: Axis-A throughput vs Axis-B substrate-profiling) is a distinct estimation discipline only thematically linked (\"structural conclusions from insufficient data\"). If a future arc creates an estimation section, §6.7.2 is the natural relocation candidate — flag it, don't cut it now. Second, the empirical anchors are durable evidence, not bloat. Keep §6 and §6.7.1 intact; revisit §6.7.2's placement only."
 },
 "8": {
  "why": "§8 governs how a seat authors any artifact a downstream agent will consume — briefs, dispatch directives, skills, activation pastes, probe sets. It is for reliability and reproducibility: jagged agents fail on sparse prompts, so the discipline forces heavy scaffolding (pre-resolved decisions, worked examples, specified failure modes, data shapes) and positive-only framing so a negative qualifier doesn't leak out-of-scope context. Without it briefs over-delegate knowably-correct picks, leak resources past task-scoping, and silently skip deploy-list wiring or fallback-path probes — each a documented empirical failure the section was accreted to prevent.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE — and it is the right call, but it is a big section that needs real trimming, not a light touch. Keep the two irreducible-judgment cores intact: §8.1 (positive-only framing) and §8.2's five rules + bidirectional-translation principle are load-bearing value-judgment that no structure can run for you. The cuts: every subsection carries a redundant \"Universality:\" clause (§8.2 literally says \"same as §8.1\") — collapse those into one sentence in the §8 preamble (~8 lines saved). §8.4 (install.sh deploy-plan smoke beat) is largely MECHANICAL — its dry-run-grep beats belong encoded as an arc Phase-C check or the validate-spec skill; slim the prose to a stub pointing there. The §8.2 \"Framing\" and \"bidirectional-translation\" paragraphs overlap on the same \"models can't close the loop alone\" point — merge to one. Rough cut: ~130 lines to ~80, zero loss of judgment. Cross-§ dependency: §8.1 leans on §7.5 scoping; §8.5/8.6 lean on §6.4/6.8 CATO discipline — preserve those refs when trimming."
 },
 "9": {
  "why": "§9 documents how bw physically stores tickets: on a `beadwork` orphan git branch, not in any local directory. It exists to prevent two destructive failure modes — a false-negative read (\"looks uninitialized\") that triggers a `bw init` over a live store, and a false-positive that produces confusing operation errors. It also encodes the hard \"never `git checkout beadwork`\" rule (which pollutes the main worktree with untracked orphan-branch files) and a Windows-worktree `worktreeconfig` recovery procedure. Without it, every seat that touches bw — POLYBIUS, PLINY, every CAPTAIN — could corrupt the durable substrate the whole architecture rests on.",
  "rec": "KEEP, but not the whole section as-is — this is a clean KEEP only because of how it's structured. The storage-model facts (orphan-branch location, detection order, the never-checkout rule) are genuinely load-bearing, universal, and tight: every seat touches bw and a wrong move corrupts the substrate. Keep those verbatim. The Windows `worktreeconfig` block (lines 13–27) is the soft spot: it was already self-debloated by the 2026-05-08 \"structurally resolved upstream\" status note demoting the three-command fix to a rare recovery procedure. That's the right move — keep the recovery fix but leave it demoted; do NOT re-promote it. Dependency: §9.1 (substrate freshness) and `MAJOR_POLYBIUS.md` §7.5/§14 already carry the project-tier framing, so §9 should stay the team-wide layer and not duplicate their cadence detail. Net: KEEP, zero re-encoding warranted."
 },
 "10": {
  "why": "§10 defines the two-axis vocabulary for HOW the PRINCIPAL participates in the team's flow: HITL (active participant, chat-first) vs Autonomous (exception-handler, bw-first), held orthogonal to MAJOR_POLYBIUS §12's Mode 1/Mode 2 (WHAT the team is doing). It encodes the trigger-word grammar (bare vs qualified), propagation rules (dispatch-boundary only, per-seat supersedes global), the universal escalation triggers, and the maturity progression Mode 2+HITL → Mode 1 → semi-autonomous. Without it, \"go autonomous\" / \"step back\" have no defined scope or propagation, regression to pair-programming reads as failure, and seats can't tell when PRINCIPAL attention is structurally required vs optional. It is the shared language every seat uses to know whether the human is in the loop right now.",
  "rec": "KEEP — this is load-bearing architectural vocabulary, not a mechanical process that could be encoded as a workflow or cron. It defines terms every seat resolves at runtime against PRINCIPAL chat signals (\"go autonomous\", \"stay HITL with POLYBIUS_X\"); there is nothing to mechanize because the trigger is a human utterance, not a schedulable event. The §11 checklist already encodes the autonomous-mode SETUP mechanics, and §10 correctly cross-refs to it without duplicating — the source-of-truth discipline is already clean (line 53 explicitly refuses to re-tabulate trigger words). The progression canon (stoa--ntn) is recently-landed off-gate canon still accreting evidence, so it is too young to cut. One honest caveat: §10 leans long for a definitions section and overlaps conceptually with MAJOR_POLYBIUS §13. If a future pass wants a trim, the worked-composition parenthetical (line 42) and the regression paragraph (line 55) are the only candidates — but both carry real anti-confusion value, so KEEP stands."
 },
 "11": {
  "why": "§11 is the entry checklist a seat runs the moment it goes autonomous: stand up the polling cron, the +144h renewal cron that beats the documented 168h cron-expiry, the radio-check handshake, the write-boundary/propagation conventions, and the mode-declaration plumbing. Without it an autonomous seat either never starts polling, or its cron silently expires on a multi-day engagement and the peer loses contact with no escalation — the engagement goes dark and the PRINCIPAL finds out late. It prevents the silent-death failure mode of long-running unattended work and guarantees a clean, consent-mediated re-bootstrap after any session-lifecycle reset.",
  "rec": "ENCODE — and §11 is already HALF-encoded: Arc 47 relocated the whole body to a CONDITIONAL module (`substrate/modules/autonomous-mode-setup.md`), leaving only the §11 stub + §0.5 index row. The encode step finishes the job by turning the protocol into a RUNNING structure, the same move §7 made. Steps 1/1.5 are pure cron machinery — CronCreate the polling cron, then the +144h one-shot renewal cron, the 4-step terminating-shape setup dance, and the cadence-switch lock-step. None of that should be read-and-executed by hand; it belongs in the cron prompt bodies themselves (`templates/polling-cron-prompt-template.md` already carries the renewal slots), with the module slimmed to a stub that names the templates and the durable-bug #40228 acceptance. Honest caveat: steps 2–9 (radio-check, write-boundary, mode-declaration/propagation) are JUDGMENT/convention, not mechanism — keep those in the module. So encode is partial, and it DEPENDS on the polling-cron template and §7's coordination module, not a clean full delete."
 },
 "12": {
  "why": "§12 is the canonical bw operations reference — the single source of truth every bw-using seat (POLYBIUS, PLINY, CAPTAINs, pair-programmer Majors) reads instead of re-deriving syntax. It prevents the recurring fumble-recovery tax: dep-direction confusion (`blocked-by` rejected), the positional-vs-`-m` comment trap, and freelanced wrong-shape `.git/config` fixes. The empirical anchor (stoa--v2o) measures ~3-5K tokens per arc lost to these fumbles. Without it, every seat rediscovers the same gotchas by trial-and-error, and the role files would each duplicate (and drift on) the syntax.",
  "rec": "The proposed ENCODE is largely WRONG for this section, so I set revisedDisposition=keep. §12 is a command-syntax reference, not a mechanical process you can hand to a workflow/hook/cron. There's no recurring trigger to fire — agents consult it ad hoc when constructing a bw call, so the \"name the structure that runs it\" move has no target. The narrow encodable sliver is the error table (§12.2): a pre-commit or wrapper that intercepts `bw comment <id> -m` and `bw dep add ... blocked-by` could mechanically prevent two of the four fumbles, slimming those rows to stubs. But that's an additive guardrail, not a reason to delete §12.2 — TIRO and read-only CAPTAINs still need the human-readable fix. The body is already tight, deduplicated by design (\"Role files reference this section; do not duplicate\"), and load-bearing. Keep it; optionally spawn a separate arc for the two-fumble lint wrapper."
 },
 "13": {
  "why": "This discipline prevents a hard crash: on Windows, agent-authored Python scripts default to cp1252 stdout, so printing any non-ASCII character (Greek theta from PDFs, em-dashes, accented citation strings) raises UnicodeEncodeError and kills the gauntlet probe mid-run. It is for making Python invocations encoding-safe by default rather than relying on each agent to remember per-script. Without it, ad-hoc helper scripts written by PLINY/VERA/ADA during a Windows gauntlet randomly die on the first non-ASCII print — the exact Batch-G smoke-test failure that anchored the section.",
  "rec": "ENCODE — but as a deterministic environment default, not a remembered per-script habit. The trigger is fully mechanical (`os.name == 'nt'`), so the right structure is a session-start hook (or settings.json env block) on Windows workspaces that exports `PYTHONUTF8=1` into the agent's bash environment. That single env write covers every downstream Python invocation, making the per-script `sys.stdout.reconfigure` line redundant for the common case. The prose then slims to a stub: \"On Windows, the substrate sets PYTHONUTF8=1 at session start (see <hook>); the per-machine `setx` and in-code reconfigure remain as belt-and-suspenders for environments the hook doesn't cover.\" Keep the empirical anchor (sh7, Batch G) as one line. Honest nuance: the per-machine `setx` genuinely cannot be encoded (it's a one-time PRINCIPAL environment write), and the in-code reconfigure protects binaries that run outside the hook'd session — so encode the agent-discipline half and retain those two as named fallbacks rather than deleting them."
 },
 "14": {
  "why": "This discipline ensures that when a dispatching seat kills a sub-agent mid-dispatch (by watchdog or manually), the JSONL transcript is captured before the process exits, because it is the only direct evidence of what the agent was doing at stall time. It also binds the read side: a post-mortem must consult that transcript when one exists rather than reconstructing from screenshots or memory. Without it, stalls leave no diagnostic trace — the empirical anchor is the ariadne DAEDALUS rev3 case where 6+ stalls had to be reconstructed from intermittent screenshots, losing the actual failure evidence.",
  "rec": "Honest correction up front: the §14 line in operating-disciplines.md is ALREADY a debloated stub (Arc 47, ticket stoa--xyb.8) — 4 lines pointing to a CONDITIONAL module plus a §0.5 index row. There is nothing left to consolidate in the slim core; that work shipped. So CONSOLIDATE as applied to the section is effectively moot — lean KEEP on the stub as-is. The only live consolidation target is the MODULE (substrate/modules/sub-agent-transcript-discipline.md, ~20 lines), where the write-side rule (line 9) and read-side rule (line 13) both re-state \"transcript is first-line evidence, reconstruction is fallback.\" Merging those two paragraphs saves ~4 lines with zero loss. Caveat: the module already carries an unresolved open question (line 15, platform-capability-dependent, tracked at stoa--dyb Item 2) — do NOT trim that; it is live judgment, not redundancy. Cross-§ dependency: §0.5 relocation index and MAJOR_PLINY.md §5.3 watchdog."
 },
 "15": {
  "why": "Section 15 is the verifier-discipline framework: it makes a verifying CAPTAIN (VERA, CATO, ARGUS, ZENO) classify every claim into a verify-difficulty 2x2 BEFORE choosing a strategy, so strategy follows from the claim's tractability rather than the verifier's energy. Its core job is to prevent the verifier-spins-forever failure mode — an agent attempting exhaustive verification of an NP-hard/undecidable claim, burning unbounded budget and never returning. It also names the cheap-catch quadrant (hard-detect/easy-verify) where the 2026-05-12 STRABO fabrication hid. Without it, intractable claims have no first-class verdict shape: the verifier either hangs or fails closed, and operators lose the honest \"this is unverifiable, here's what I bounded-checked\" signal needed for disposition.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE is right, but a light one — this section is load-bearing and mostly tight. The redundancy is between §15.3 (four strategies, prose) and §15.6 (worked examples) and §15.7: the same quadrant logic is restated three times. Concrete cuts: (1) §15.5's \"Rationale for N=10\" carries three numbered sub-arguments plus a parallel prose paragraph above it — merge to ~4 lines, the asymmetric-cost point is the keeper. (2) Fold §15.7's self-referential paragraph into a single sentence; its value is \"doc arcs are mostly easy-easy,\" not the full re-derivation. (3) §15.6 examples 1/2/4 each re-explain their quadrant after §15.3 already did — trim the re-explanation, keep the evidence (commit SHAs, the universal-quantifier case). Roughly 75 lines to ~50, zero rule loss. KEEP §15.1–15.4 intact: the 2x2, the discipline rule, and the two verdict shapes are the irreducible core. Dependency: the INCOMPLETE/UNVERIFIABLE schema in §15.4 is enforced by the save-verdict skill — do not slim §15.4's required-field lists, since the skill validates against them."
 },
 "16": {
  "why": "The bw-fit matrix exists to stop a POLYBIUS seat from reflexively reaching for bw as the universal substrate on every new project. It records empirically-observed scaling characteristics (bw's TreeFS.Commit rewrites the whole tree per commit, going superlinear past ~5k tickets) and pairs them with a layered-architecture answer: bw for the write side, Ariadne/hypergraph for read-side projection. Without it, a future project with a 10k+ knowledge-graph or bulk-ingest shape gets deployed onto bw, hits the scaling wall mid-arc, and has to be re-platformed — the exact failure the stoa+ariadne 2026-05 integration arcs paid to discover.",
  "rec": "Honest correction: the §16 entry in operating-disciplines.md is ALREADY a 5-line stub — Arc 47 relocated the substance to .claude/modules/bw-fit-matrix.md (CONDITIONAL) with provenance + a §0.5 relocation-index row. There is nothing left to consolidate in core canon; the stub is maximally slim. So CONSOLIDATE is overstated for the section as written. If the target is the MODULE, it's already tight (~37 lines, three distinct parts: matrix, layered framing, decision rule). The one squeezable redundancy: §16.3's decision rule mostly re-walks the §16.1 matrix rows in prose. Fold the decision rule into a one-line preamble above the matrix (\"walk the rows; right-choice = deploy, NOT-bw = use the named alternative, hybrid = §16.2 layering\") and drop the numbered list — roughly 8 lines to 3, zero loss. Cross-§ dependency: any edit must keep the §0.5 index row and stoa--vmc anchor intact."
 },
 "17": {
  "why": "§17 bundles two disciplines that surface together for a Stoa-deployed project: (1) the fork-over-upstream default — for an AI agent team, forking-and-patching a dependency is cheap while filing an upstream issue is expensive coordination overhead, so the calculus inverts the human-dev convention; and (2) the agent-time latency budget — when traffic is 100% agent-to-system, the budget is per-LLM-turn (5-20s) not per-human-keystroke (<1s), so optimizing for human-perceived-instant response is over-optimization. Without them, a new project silently inherits human-dev reflexes: it files upstream issues that block the roadmap instead of forking, and it burns effort on async-queue plumbing and UI polish a no-human-in-the-loop system never needs.",
  "rec": "Reading the real text downgrades CONSOLIDATE: §17 is ALREADY debloated. Arc 47 (ticket stoa--xyb.8) relocated the entire body to the conditional module `modules/oss-dep-and-latency.md`; what remains in operating-disciplines.md is a 5-line stub plus a relocation pointer and a §0.5 index row. There is essentially nothing canon-resident left to trim. So the honest disposition for the canon section is KEEP-the-stub (revisedDisposition: keep). If \"consolidate\" is aimed at the MODULE body, a modest cut is real but not large: §17.1's 150-LOC TreeFS example runs ~6 lines and could halve; the \"fork is not destructive\" / \"upstream stays optional\" pair (lines 22-23) restates the §17.1 thesis and can fold into one line; §17.2's 5-row table plus surrounding prose duplicate each other and could lose ~3 rows. Net module cut ~10-12 lines, zero rule loss. But these are CONDITIONAL-read material, so squeezing them buys little — this is already-tight, low-priority."
 },
 "18": {
  "why": "This discipline gives the orchestrator a way to see and steer a running CAPTAIN when Anthropic's tool surface offers no mid-execution introspection and no push-interrupt (only a hard TaskStop). It uses bw as a shared status channel: CAPTAINs heartbeat upward at every state transition (and on a 60-min floor), and read-before-write on every yield so the orchestrator's redirections land. Without it, a dispatched agent is a black box — the orchestrator cannot tell whether it is working, stuck, or dead, cannot redirect it short of killing it, and (per the ariadne PLINY incident) can even confabulate that it never dispatched at all. The stateless-agent / cold-pickup-brief half (§18.6) prevents agents from depending on a non-existent \"resume/SendMessage\" mechanism.",
  "rec": "ENCODE is only HALF right, so treat this as ENCODE-the-mechanical-tail, KEEP-the-architecture. The genuinely mechanical part is §18.5's dispatch sequence (ToolSearch -> background Agent -> materialize task_id to bw -> persistent Monitor bw-poll -> TaskStop on completion). That is already encoded once in MAJOR_PLINY.md §5.8 / MAJOR_POLYBIUS.md §7.6; §18.5 is a pointer TABLE, not duplicated prose, so the encode is mostly DONE — the running structure is the orchestrator's Monitor bw-poll loop plus the canonical bw-poll template the MAJORs own. The encode move here is small: confirm the loop runs from the MAJOR template and slim §18.5 to a one-line pointer. But §18.1-§18.4 and §18.6 are NOT mechanical — the heartbeat protocol, the 60-min floor (a calibrated value), the cooperative-yield-is-load-bearing argument, and the stateless-team architecture are irreducible judgment/canon that must stay in prose. Cross-§ dependency: §7 (the running Monitor/cron structure) and §19 (confabulation discipline, which §18.6 explicitly invokes)."
 },
 "19": {
  "why": "This discipline governs what an agent says when its internal model and the actual state disagree: it mandates \"uncertain, checking\" — an explicit first-person admission plus a concrete verification action — instead of a confident assertion either way. It prevents confabulation: a seat producing a confident false statement that PRINCIPAL or a peer then acts on as true. Without it, a single bad assertion corrupts downstream work AND degrades trust in every subsequent statement from that seat, dropping the channel's signal-to-noise ratio permanently. The §19.6/§19.7 sub-disciplines extend the same shape to attestation-time (\"cite live-verified state\") and idle-scan (\"don't claim past work as own\").",
  "rec": "KEEP. This is load-bearing architectural canon, not bloat: it is the designated substrate-canonical HOME for confabulation discipline (§3 says so explicitly), and §19.4 is the single canonical relationship-map binding it to §7.2 verify-then-execute and to specializations §19.6/§19.7. Cutting or relocating it would orphan cross-refs from MAJOR_PLINY §7.2/§5.10 and MAJOR_POLYBIUS §4.3 that point INTO it. Each subsection earns its place via a distinct empirical anchor (stoa--ioy, ezj, 53u — three real incidents, three failure shapes). It is not mechanically encodable: the core act is a JUDGMENT call about one's own epistemic state, which no hook can run. If anything, this is a CONSOLIDATE candidate, not a cut — §19.2's PRINCIPAL-intent paragraph and the §19.4-vs-§19.6.2-vs-§19.7.2 relationship statements restate the cross-§ map two or three times; merging those could reclaim ~15-20 lines with zero rule loss. But that is a trim within KEEP, not a reason to remove."
 },
 "20": {
  "why": "Credential discipline is the structural rule that agents NEVER hold credentials — routine credentialed work against any token/OAuth/service-account-gated API (Railway, gcloud, gh, op, aws, etc.) routes through CI, and agents only author the workflows CI runs. It prevents the empirically-proven failure mode that any credential in agent-reachable scope eventually surfaces: an agent under tool-call pressure will printenv, cat the file, grep, or write a debug helper and leak the secret. Without it, every workspace drifts back toward per-call credentialed access — auth-fatigue, reflexive approval, and the railway--r9z double-failure (dozens of biometric prompts plus retry-after-refusal) recur. It also encodes refusal-as-signal: a refused credentialed call means the design is wrong, not a thing to route around.",
  "rec": "KEEP, and it stays because it is load-bearing safety canon, empirically earned, and already half-debloated. The heavy detail (§20.1 WIF/secrets-manager chain, §20.5 Railway notes, §20.6 skill xref) is ALREADY relocated to the conditional module `credential-discipline-detail.md`; what remains inline is exactly the irreducible always-on core: the structural rule, the five tested-and-rejected anti-patterns (§20.2), refusal-as-signal (§20.3), and the preventive universal rule (§20.4). None of that is mechanizable away — there is no workflow/hook that can run \"an agent refuses a credentialed brief and redirects\"; that is judgment fired in-dispatch. The five anti-patterns read long but each prevents a specific re-derivation from theory, so they earn their lines. One honest note: §20.4's boundary marker against §20.3 (preventive-vs-responsive) is ~6 lines restating a distinction the two subsections already make — a future CONSOLIDATE could shave it, but it is genuinely clarifying. Net: leave as-is. Cross-§ dependency: §20.1/§20.5/§20.6 live in the module via §0.5 relocation index — keep that row."
 },
 "21": {
  "why": "§21 tells every seat to author durable artifacts (bw tickets, retros, commits, design docs, handoffs) so they retrieve cleanly both for a human re-reading after /compact and for vector search across the corpus. The four sub-disciplines — search-friendly titles, explicit cross-refs, dense self-contained chunks, and the alignment claim that authoring-for-ingestion equals authoring-for-compaction-recovery — prevent the failure where artifacts collide in search (\"fix the thing\"), reference context that has since decayed, or can't be understood as a fragment. Without it the substrate corpus degrades into low-recall noise the moment Ariadne search goes live, and post-compaction recovery gets harder for the same reasons.",
  "rec": "The proposed CUT is wrong — this reads as load-bearing authoring canon, not redundant/stale/niche text. It is also already lean: ~20 lines, four tight sub-disciplines, an explicit forward-only scope guard, and a clean empirical anchor. It applies universally to every seat that writes anything durable, so it belongs in universal canon, not a relocated module. If anything trim, not cut: the \"POLYBIUS-specific framing\" line (19) and the long empirical-anchor paragraph (21) could lose ~3-4 lines by collapsing the N=1/§6.7.1 sourcing into a one-line pointer, and the redundant restatement of \"no trade-off\" in bullet 4 (15) duplicates the alignment already in line 5. That is a CONSOLIDATE of maybe 6-8 lines, no rule lost. Dependency note: this discipline references `MAJOR_POLYBIUS.md` §16.3/§16.4 and §4.8 fix-now — any trim must keep those cross-refs intact. Net call: do not cut; lightly consolidate."
 },
 "22": {
  "why": "§22 is the discipline for absorbing a new upstream `bw` release across the three surfaces the substrate touches it on — deployment (container/Dockerfile/SHA256), substrate (canon/skills/conventions), and workspace (subprocess call-sites that shell out to bw). Without it, a `bw` version bump either gets ignored (silent regression at a `bw_ingest.py`-class call-site, or a stale deployed binary) or gets adopted credulously off the changelog — the failure the §22.2/stoa--s6n worked example exists to prevent, where bw 0.13.0's registry was LOCKED in a directive but empirically didn't work on the Windows install. It forces verify-before-lock and one-concrete-ticket-per-axis so a release lands deliberately instead of by drift or by handwave.",
  "rec": "The proposed ENCODE is only half-right, and the half that's right is already done. §22's slim-core text is a 5-line stub; the body lives in the conditional module `bw-upgrade.md`. Within that, ONLY Step 1 (Trigger) is a mechanical poll — and it is ALREADY encoded: the `check-bw-release` skill queries the GitHub releases API against a per-workspace `.bw-release-last-check` baseline and emits the 3-axis template. The honest encode action is small: leave the skill as-is, optionally wrap it in an operator-scheduled cron (the module explicitly declines a cron default per directive A7 — that's PRINCIPAL's call, not ours to bake in). Steps 2-5 (Review/File/Dispatch/Verify) are irreducible POLYBIUS judgment — verify-then-execute, axis classification, ticket-with-concrete-action — and cannot be mechanized away. So the disposition is really \"already-debloated KEEP, with Step 1 already encoded.\" Nothing more to slim. Cross-§ dependency: §6.7.1 (off-gate N=1 promotion), §4.3/§4.8 (the framings Steps 2-3 specialize)."
 },
 "23": {
  "why": "This section defines the two-layer agent model every Stoa workspace runs: a BASE team deployed from substrate (mechanically updatable via install.sh / apply.sh) plus optional CUSTOM agents the workspace owns and substrate tools never touch. It exists so that \"update the base safely all the way down\" and \"customize freely\" can both be true without collision. Without it, the substrate tools have no principled scope boundary — an install would either clobber workspace customizations or refuse to update anything, and custom CAPTAINs would silently shadow base ones. The per-class path convention (subdirectory for CAPTAINs/templates/modules, custom- prefix for skills) is the concrete contract that makes the base/custom split mechanically enforceable rather than aspirational.",
  "rec": "KEEP — this is load-bearing architecture, not prose that can be slimmed. Three reasons it stays. (1) It is the scope contract for all three substrate tools: install.sh, check.sh, apply.sh each carry cite-comments pointing here, and the §23.2 path table is the literal spec those tools implement (subdirectory vs custom- prefix). Cutting or relocating it orphans live tooling. (2) The asymmetry in §23.2 is forced by Claude Code's actual discovery behavior (agents/ recursive, skills/ single-level) — empirically web-verified, doc-cited, irreducible. That is exactly the kind of \"why this pick\" knowledge §8.2 scaffolding says to preserve. (3) Every seat reads it; it is the universal-team framing that §17 and §19 refine. It is already tight: a source-of-truth quote, one table, four per-seat rules, provenance. Do not encode (no mechanical process to run) and do not consolidate (no duplication — the POLYBIUS-specific detail already lives in §17, not here). Dependency: keep §17/§19 cross-refs intact."
 },
 "24": {
  "why": "This discipline prevents two specific git-state corruptions before a seat creates an arc-build branch: branching off a polluted base (a prior arc's branch still in flight) or off a stale main (local diverged from origin). Without the two-check rule, a new arc inherits the previous arc's uncommitted or unmerged work, or builds on commits that never reached origin/main — producing tangled diffs, lost work, and merge conflicts that are expensive to unwind mid-arc. The check is cheap (two git inspections) and the failure is costly, so it runs before every `git checkout -b`.",
  "rec": "Reconsider ENCODE for §24 itself — but the encode belongs at the canon home, not here. §24 is ALREADY a thin cross-ref: its substantive body lives at `MAJOR_PLINY.md` §5.9, and §24 only restates the two checks plus a \"why thin cross-ref\" rationale. The two checks (no other arc-build branch in flight; local main = origin/main) ARE mechanical and should be encoded as a pre-branch hook or a step in the arc-build activation flow — but that encode is a §5.9 action, not a §24 one. What §24 should DO: stay a stub. Slim it to the two-check summary plus the cross-ref list; the \"why thin cross-ref / future-promotion\" paragraph (lines 9–10) is meta-justification that can compress to one sentence, cutting ~10 lines. Cross-§ dependency: the real encode target is `MAJOR_PLINY.md` §5.9; §24 cannot be encoded independently of it."
 },
 "25": {
  "why": "PRINCIPAL-gate discipline draws a line between two things the substrate used to conflate: whether a step structurally requires PRINCIPAL input (authorization) versus when routine work should surface to PRINCIPAL (cadence). It declares that a design clause naming PRINCIPAL as the deciding seat is a BLOCK — autonomous mode pauses and waits when PRINCIPAL is AFK, it does not proceed past the gate. Without it, an autonomous workflow reads a gate clause as a cadence-relaxable marker and crosses it unauthorized — the exact Arc 26 / stoa--501 sector-4 unauthorized-mutation failure that prompted the rule.",
  "rec": "KEEP, but with one honest caveat. This stays because it is architectural and load-bearing: it carves the authorization axis cleanly away from §10/§11's cadence axis, names the empirically-anchored failure it prevents (Arc 26 / stoa--501), and is densely cross-wired into three seat envelopes (DAEDALUS §6.7, ADA §5.8, VERA §5.10) plus two templates. Those dependencies make it the universal locus the seats inherit from; collapsing it would re-open the conflation. The caveat: §25.5's --no-local deep-dive (25 lines on CVE-2024-32020, --shared, --local --no-hardlinks tradeoffs) is genuinely encyclopedic and is the one squeezable part — it is a how-to-clone-safely recipe, not gate discipline. Consider relocating that block to a probe-isolation reference module, leaving a one-line pointer. The gate rule itself is already tight and stays whole."
 },
 "26": {
  "why": "This discipline keeps stray scheduled crons from silently re-firing in freshly activated seats. When a seat is paste-activated in a fresh terminal, an old CronCreate from a prior occupant of that terminal/session could still be live; the hygiene preamble forces the new session to run CronList and CronDelete any cron before doing substantive work. Without it, a zombie cron from a previous arc keeps polling, posting, or self-triggering against stale context — burning tokens and injecting confusing, out-of-band activity that nobody owns. It prevents cron-leakage across seat handoffs in the same terminal lineage.",
  "rec": "The encode mechanism already exists and §26 should NOT be re-encoded — the proposed disposition is half-right but mislabels the work. The CronList/CronDelete preamble is already a running structure: it is baked into every activation paste via the `{{CRON_HYGIENE_CLAUSE}}` slot in `substrate/templates/paste-instruction-template.md`, so the seat executes it mechanically on activation rather than reading-and-remembering. The substantive canon already lives at `MAJOR_POLYBIUS.md` §5.1.3. So §26 in operating-disciplines.md is ALREADY a thin stub — three sentences plus a \"why thin cross-ref\" justification plus a cross-ref list. The honest move is CONSOLIDATE-to-stub: cut the ~5-line \"Why thin cross-ref, not full universal-team mirror\" paragraph (lines 7) down to one sentence, since it re-argues a point the cross-ref list already implies, trimming ~18 lines to ~8. Depends on §5.1.3 and the template remaining the source of truth."
 },
 "27": {
  "why": "This discipline names WHERE intelligence lives in a script-based workflow: mechanical scripts stay narrow and deterministic; recognition-of-novel-strangeness moves to an LLM-grade inspection agent run AFTER the script; POLYBIUS triages the findings against the §25 PRINCIPAL-gate. It prevents the \"make-script-comprehensive\" failure mode — the anchor is Arc 26 ballooning check.sh from 489 to 893 lines chasing every known-strangeness category. Without it, scripts accrete unbounded recognition logic, become unreviewable and brittle, yet still miss the novel strangeness a script was never pre-programmed to notice. It is an architecture-axis rule, deliberately distinct from the cadence axis (§10/§11) and the gate axis (§25).",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE is the right verb but ALREADY DONE in the canon — the live §27 is a 4-line relocation stub (operating-disciplines.md:1120-1127); the full ~80-line discipline was relocated to .claude/modules/mechanical-inspection-split.md in Arc 47. So there is nothing left to trim in the slim-core residue; squeezing the stub would only damage substrate citers who reference §27.2 by name. The remaining consolidation opportunity lives IN THE MODULE, not the canon: §27.6 and §27.7 restate the N=1/§6.7.1-off-gate framing and the §25/§19.6/§5.10 future-integration partners twice over, and §27.1 plus §27.6 both narrate the 2026-05-16 PRINCIPAL declaration with overlapping provenance. Merging those two provenance passes and the duplicated cross-ref list would cut roughly 12-15 lines from the module with zero rule loss. Cross-§ dependency: the §25 gate is the triage partner (Step 3) and must NOT be folded in — conflating gate-axis with architecture-axis is explicitly rejected at §27.7."
 },
 "28": {
  "why": "§28 keeps a workspace's git identity uniform (commit Author stays the PRINCIPAL's configured identity, per the absolute \"never override Author:\" rule) while still recording WHICH agent seat actually authored each commit, via a Co-Authored-By trailer. It prevents misattribution: without it, a reading agent doing git blame sees PRINCIPAL on every line and asserts human authorship of work an agent actually did — the exact 2026-05-04 ariadne--xft.4 ARGUS git-blame incident this discipline is anchored to. Without the trailer there is no read-side signal at all to trace a line back to its seat through the squash-merge-into-main lifecycle.",
  "rec": "Honest call: the proposed ENCODE is only PARTIALLY right, and the text shows it. §28 is two different things glued together. The trailer-EMISSION is genuinely mechanical and encodable — the string format (§28.1), the tagged/not-tagged scope (§28.2), and the squash-merge --body pitfall (§28.3.1) are deterministic rules a pre-commit hook or PLINY ship-checklist script can enforce, letting that prose slim to a stub pointing at the enforcing structure (already half-done via MAJOR_PLINY.md §5.10/§5.12 and CAPTAIN_ADA.md §5.5). But §28.5 (the read-side \"do NOT infer human authorship from git blame\") is irreducible JUDGMENT a reading agent applies — it cannot be hooked; it must stay as canon. So: ENCODE the emission half (hook + stub), KEEP §28.5 as prose. Cross-§ dependency: §19.6 (sister \"cite live-verified state\") and §25 (the gate that adjudicated this). Don't oversell encode as covering the whole section."
 },
 "29": {
  "why": "§29 defines how multiple Stoa-deployed workspaces coexist without colliding: prefix-namespaces keep each team's bw tickets distinct, cross-team request routing says where a request lands, consumed-artifact channels define how one team's output reaches another, and team discovery lets a seat find its peers. It is the outermost of three nested coordination layers (§7 within a team, MAJOR_POLYBIUS §19 within a workspace, §29 across workspaces). Without it, two teams sharing infrastructure would clash on ticket IDs, misroute cross-team requests, and have no defined way to find or consume each other's work — silent collisions and dropped hand-offs.",
  "rec": "The disposition is mis-scaled: §29 is ALREADY debloated. Its entire body was relocated to a CONDITIONAL module (`.claude/modules/multi-team-interop.md`) via the §33 relocation mechanism; what remains in universal canon is a 7-line stub — a one-line pointer, the three-layer nesting note (§7 → POLYBIUS §19 → §29), and a relocation-index reference. There is essentially nothing left to consolidate. Treat this as effective KEEP: the stub is already minimal and load-bearing (it is the discovery breadcrumb that tells a seat the module exists). The only squeeze available is cosmetic — the parenthetical topic list in line 3 partially duplicates what the module's own intro states, so trimming it saves ~1 line at the cost of a useful at-a-glance index. Not worth it. Honest revised call: KEEP. CONSOLIDATE presumes inline prose to merge that no longer exists here; the real consolidation work belongs inside the relocated module, not in this stub. Cross-§ dependency: §33 (relocation machinery) and §0.5 (relocation index) own this section's real footprint."
 },
 "30": {
  "why": "The four-layer model is the substrate's vocabulary for what an agent's identity actually IS: role file (universal seat identity), memories (per-PRINCIPAL alignment), handoff (session-continuity), and bw substrate (durable detail). It exists so seats reason correctly about WHAT crosses a compaction or a generational handoff and WHERE each kind of knowledge lives. Without it, agents confabulate \"what I know about you\" from the in-context window instead of reading memories, inline durable detail into handoffs (collapsing them into transcripts), and lose the principle that different PRINCIPALs producing different behaviors is alignment working — not noise. It is the conceptual backbone the handoff-author skill, memory-introspection, and generational-lineage architecture all rest on.",
  "rec": "KEEP — and note it is ALREADY debloated. §30 in the canon is a 4-line relocation stub (Arc 47 moved the body to a CONDITIONAL module, `modules/four-layer-identity.md`, with a §0.5 index row at line 61). So \"KEEP\" really means keep the stub-plus-conditional-module shape; there is nothing left to cut in the canon. It stays because it is architectural, not mechanical: it cannot ENCODE (no workflow/hook/cron runs a \"model\" — it is the vocabulary seats reason WITH), and it cannot CUT (it is load-bearing for the handoff-author skill, memory-introspection behavior, and the generational-lineage architecture in SPECIFICATION §10.1). Dependency note: it cross-references §16 (session lifecycle), §29 (multi-team), §10, and §6.7.1 — and §30.5 honestly flags N=1 provenance, off-gate on PRINCIPAL authority. Do nothing further; the conditional-module split already did the debloat."
 },
 "31": {
  "why": "§31 captures two empirically-surfaced design principles for any artifact a workspace ships to a peer workspace: the agent-installable distribution model (AI is the primary reader who answers \"do I need this?\", human is decision-authority) and composability framing (breadth = one substrate many wirings, not a pile of demos). It prevents two failure modes: authoring component onboarding materials for human-first reading when the AI is the real first reader, and overselling breadth via demo-inventory that ages out the moment a user's use case differs. Without it, substrate components (Stoa itself, Ariadne Core, future Railway skills) get marketed and shaped wrong, and the base-vs-custom composability story gets lost.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE is right but mild — the main-file §31 is already a 5-line stub (relocated to the CONDITIONAL module `.claude/modules/substrate-component-design.md` via §33, indexed at §0.5 rows 63-64), so nothing remains to cut there. The squeeze is inside the ~1521-word module, which carries real redundancy: §31.1's \"Worked instance — Stoa substrate\" and §31.3's \"N=2 — Stoa substrate\" restate the same SKILL.md-routes-to-skills + install.sh facts; §31.2's Ariadne and Stoa \"Concretely\" paragraphs each re-explain one-substrate-many-wirings; and the N=2 honest-scope rationale appears in both the intro and §31.3. Merge the duplicated worked-instances into one Stoa block and one Ariadne block, trim the doubled N=2 framing: roughly 1521 → ~1150 words, no principle lost. Honest caveat: this is module-internal tightening, not main-file debloat, and the gain is modest. Dependency: relocation is owned by §33; the §0.5 index rows must stay in sync if §31.3's provenance line moves."
 },
 "32": {
  "why": "§32 captures a concrete test-environment failure mode: jsdom does not drive `requestAnimationFrame`, so animation-library exit animations (`motion`/`framer-motion` `AnimatePresence mode=\"popLayout\"`) never complete — the element lingers in the DOM at `opacity:0`. The discipline prevents a specific false test failure: asserting \"element absent from DOM\" fails forever under jsdom even when the real-browser behavior is correct. Without it, test authors either write brittle presence assertions that flake, or — worse — weaken a qualitative-acceptance audit to make a green bar, smoothing over the environment quirk as if it were a real defect. The fix is the disjunctive \"absent OR opacity:0\" assertion plus a shared helper so individual tests don't re-derive it.",
  "rec": "CUT is right in spirit but already half-executed, so the honest action is narrow. §32 is NOT inline prose — Arc 47 already relocated the full discipline to `.claude/modules/jsdom-timing-discipline.md` as CONDITIONAL, leaving only a 5-line stub plus the §0.5 relocation-index row. The content is genuinely project-specific (the-stoa's React/Vite app, `motion`/`framer-motion`, jsdom) and has no place in *universal* substrate canon — that part of CUT is correct. But the module is real, empirically anchored, and consumed by three CAPTAIN seats (ADA §5.9, VERA §5.1, CATO), so don't delete the module. Concrete move: keep the disk module, but CUT the §32 core stub entirely and let the §0.5 relocation-index row be the sole core pointer — that removes the redundant double-pointer. DEPENDENCY: must update §0.5 and the three CAPTAIN cross-refs in the same edit, or you orphan live citations."
 },
 "33": {
  "why": "§33 names the substrate's core memory architecture: instructions are a composable library delivered at dispatch time, not all-memorized by every agent. It defines the three delivery channels (inline, disk module, bw ticket), the three relocation classes that give debloated content a lossless home (CONDITIONAL→module, PROVENANCE→bw, DUPLICATE→consolidate), and the rule that the routing map and relocation index stay inline in the orchestrator core. Without it, the entire debloat method has no destination — \"slim the core\" becomes \"delete content,\" and an orchestrator has no principled way to decide what an agent gets and through which channel. It prevents both bloat (everything memorized) and loss (content cut with nowhere to land).",
  "rec": "KEEP — this is the load-bearing meta-section that the entire debloat exercise depends on; it is the spec for HOW every other section gets debloated, so cutting or relocating it would be self-undermining. It is also already maximally tight: ~20 lines of pure mechanism, every line load-bearing, with the full procedure and templates already relocated to `.claude/modules/README.md` (line 23) — §33 has itself eaten its own debloat method. The one structural constraint it correctly self-documents is the anti-recursion rule (lines 15-18): the routing map and relocation index must stay INLINE, never become a module, because \"an index that must itself be loaded-on-demand never fires.\" Do not touch that. Cross-§ dependency: §33 is the relocation target named by §7 and §34's debloat writeups (the CONDITIONAL→module move), so it must land before they do. Leave the prose as-is; there is nothing to squeeze without losing a load-bearing clause."
 },
 "35": {
  "why": "§35 binds every security mitigation to the named threat it was built to defeat, BEFORE build, so a mitigation cannot silently drift to a plausible-but-wrong surface. It prevents the origindex-trw failure: a correctly-named threat (M2), an ambiguous ratification, a build that picked the easier wrong surface, and five verify stages that all asked \"does it work?\" and none \"does it defeat M2?\" — caught only at the close-gate. Without it, \"verify the artifact behaves as built\" can pass while the artifact defeats nothing; threat coverage is assumed rather than checked, and the drift is invisible until something is exploited.",
  "rec": "KEEP — this is load-bearing architecture and, critically, just-shipped (Arc 52 Arc A, ratified 2026-05-31). It stays because it is the prevention spine that no other section provides: §35 is the only place a threat-ID (M<n>) is minted, restated unconditionally (A1 keystone), folded into design (A2), and mapped to attack-path/how-defeated (A3). Each sub-section maps to a distinct seat duty (PLINY §5.13, DAEDALUS §3/§6.12, ARGUS §6.9), so cutting prose would orphan live cross-refs. It is also already-tight: §35.5's honest-claim and self-reference carve-out are hard-won precision (named-residual scoping, ARGUS-confirmed carve-out) that a debloat pass would damage, not improve. Dependency: §36 consumes §35's outputs (remediation layer); §35 is detection-within-arc. Honest caveat: N=1 provenance (§35.7) means this is provisional canon — keep, but resist further generalization until evidence accretes."
 },
 "36": {
  "why": "§36 is the RESPONSE layer of the threat-defeat stack: when §35/Arc-B detects a named threat `M<n>` that shipped without a passing threat-coverage binding, §36 dictates that the gauntlet STOPS, surfaces to the PRINCIPAL, and escalates the fix into a DEDICATED, goal-locked remediation arc whose single deliverable is \"defeat M<n>.\" It prevents remediation-drift: the documented failure (origindex-trw M2) where a threat-fix rode as a sub-item of a larger build, competed with the \"real\" deliverable, and took the easier reading. Without §36, a detected security gap gets inline-patched on the originating arc, where there is a larger deliverable to hide it under — and the fix drifts exactly as M2 did.",
  "rec": "KEEP, and it stays for three converging reasons. (1) It is JUST-SHIPPED — Arc 60 (stoa--h2z), self-applied in the most recent commit (236af86); a section this fresh has no accreted bloat to squeeze. (2) It is the keystone of an architectural triad: §35 prevents, Arc-B detects, §36 responds — cutting it leaves a detect-with-no-named-response hole. (3) It is HONESTLY SCOPED already: §36.4 explicitly refuses to overclaim \"structurally cannot drift,\" §36.5 distinguishes authoring-effort from missing-infra (the very error my own memory flags as web-unverified and that this section corrects), and §36.6 bounds the claim to named threats. Dependencies are load-bearing, not redundant: §36.2's spawn-gate IS the §25 PRINCIPAL-gate; §36.4's seat-side net IS §6 redundancy. The one future move — the Tier-2 executable /defeat-threat workflow — is correctly deferred IN the text (§36.5), not deletable now. Do not consolidate: the trigger table, the verbatim STOP payload, and the goal-lock honesty are each doing distinct work."
 },
 "1": {
  "why": "Agents inherit a strong training bias toward shipping fast — declaring done, minimizing round-trips, resisting more work. In a correctness-critical multi-agent system that bias causes premature completion and skipped verification. §1 is one of five explicit counter-pressures (with MVP, gold-plating, passivity, plausible-citation) that re-set the default from move-fast to be-thorough. It is a stance, not a procedure.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE — but as a DILEMMA, your call. §1 through §5 are five separate suppressor sections all saying one root thing: resist the trained move-fast / minimize-effort bias. I would merge them into ONE Anti-pattern-stance section (five named pressures, five lines), cutting four section-headers and the repeated framing while keeping each named pressure. The risk that makes it a dilemma: these are load-bearing culture — the team's whole value is resisting these pressures, and a merged version might read as less emphatic. So the call is yours: how much emphasis is worth how much length? My lean is merge, since five scattered sections is itself a kind of bloat and one tight stance can be more emphatic — but I will not make that value-call for you."
 },
 "7": {
  "why": "When a user-tier POLYBIUS and a project-tier floor-manager POLYBIUS run at the same time, they share no memory and have no presence channel — they coordinate ONLY through bw (polling, tagged comments, escalation). §7 is the protocol that makes that async coordination work: polling cadence, radio-check liveness, from/for tag-attribution, cross-tier write boundaries, surface-vs-stay-quiet. Without it, two POLYBIUS seats either collide or go silent — exactly the failures we hit early this session.",
  "rec": "ENCODE — specifically as a recurring Monitor/cron-prompt, not a one-shot workflow. §7 is ALREADY half-debloated: its body was relocated to a conditional module via §33, so op-disc holds only stubs. The encode step turns the protocol into a RUNNING structure — a cron whose self-contained prompt embeds the whole protocol (tickets, cadence, tag-parsing, surface criteria, escalation). The agent does not read-the-module-and-remember-to-poll; the cron fires the poll with the protocol baked in. You watched this all session: the round-coordination Monitor IS §7 encoded. Result: §7 prose stays a stub-pointer; the operational form is the cron-prompt template. Honest nuance: surface-vs-quiet and escalation are JUDGMENT — encode the scaffolding and relocate the judgment INTO the trigger payload (per §34), do not delete it. So this encode depends on §34."
 },
 "34": {
  "why": "Harness-fired triggers (hook reasons, Stop reasons, PostToolUse additionalContext, cron prompts) are how the system re-injects a rule at the moment of action — AFTER an agent may have compacted away the discipline doc. §34 requires every trigger payload to be self-contained (state WHY it fired and WHAT to do, never a bare pointer) — because a compacted agent literally cannot follow a see-section pointer to text it no longer holds. It is the convention that makes the whole trigger/enforcement layer survive compaction.",
  "rec": "CONSOLIDATE — a small one, leaning KEEP. §34 was already debloated once (Arc 46), so it is already tight. The only redundancy: the compaction-rationale is stated twice (para 1 and para 2). Merge them and ~12 lines becomes ~6, with zero loss of the rule. Reading the actual text down-graded my own confidence — this is borderline KEEP, not a meaningful consolidate. The real lesson: already-debloated sections like this have almost nothing left to squeeze; the gains are in sections prior arcs never touched."
 }
};
